{"name":"Misty Mountains","hex":"#c0d0e6","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"misty","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Covered in mist; foggy.","Dim; vague; obscure."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/misty"},{"term":"mountains","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of mountain","mountain range"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mountains"}],"entries":[{"title":"Geography of Middle-earth","blocks":[{"text":"The geography of Middle-earth encompasses the physical, political, and moral geography of J. R. R. Tolkien's fictional continent Middle-earth on the planet Arda, but widely taken to mean all of creation (Eä) as well as all of his writings about it. Arda was created as a flat world, incorporating a Western continent, Aman, which became the home of the godlike Valar, as well as Middle-earth. At the end of the First Age, the Western part of Middle-earth, Beleriand, was drowned in the War of Wrath. In the Second Age, a large island, Númenor, was created in the Great Sea, Belegaer, between Aman and Middle-earth; it was destroyed in a cataclysm near the end of the Second Age, in which Arda was remade as a spherical world, and Aman was removed so that Men could not reach it."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geography_of_Middle-earth","wikidata":"Q105436668"},{"title":"Mountain","blocks":[{"text":"A mountain is an elevated portion of the surface of a planet, generally with steep sides that show significant exposed bedrock. Although there are no universally accepted definitions, a mountain is usually considered higher than a hill. By definition, mountains are often thought of as being a landform which is higher than 2,000 feet (610 m). It may either have a limited summit area or be a smaller plateau with high elevation and steep sides. A few mountains are isolated summits, but most occur in mountain ranges."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Everest_North_Face_toward_Base_Camp_Tibet_Luca_Galuzzi_2006.jpg/330px-Everest_North_Face_toward_Base_Camp_Tibet_Luca_Galuzzi_2006.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mountain","wikidata":"Q8502"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/75/Fonstad_Mount_Doom_detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/75/Fonstad_Mount_Doom_detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Fonstad created \"the most comprehensive set\" of thematic maps of Middle-earth, such as Frodo and Sam's route to Mount Doom to destroy the One Ring.[13]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFonstad_Mount_Doom_detail.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Idylls_of_the_King_18.jpg/500px-Idylls_of_the_King_18.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Idylls_of_the_King_18.jpg/500px-Idylls_of_the_King_18.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tolkien borrowed the Arthurian place-name Brocéliande for an early version of Beleriand.[32] 1868 illustration by Gustave Doré","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIdylls_of_the_King_18.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Volc%C3%A1n_Chimborazo%2C_%22El_Taita_Chimborazo%22_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Volc%C3%A1n_Chimborazo%2C_%22El_Taita_Chimborazo%22_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Volc%C3%A1n_Chimborazo%2C_%22El_Taita_Chimborazo%22_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-Volc%C3%A1n_Chimborazo%2C_%22El_Taita_Chimborazo%22_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_conte